tƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkf200000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;EbNCfGheCDQqjosGbO2akD44NBUKEtQHvkW5tY8qgecƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarks0.31597169999999997ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zXjRd7_yb6UKD1lxlaBIA755pxzKQjLCjqFfq7b4Dmcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka4ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;N7fjlILdJgAjLDrV6FPBgz-ldpz87Q_AmSiEKyyyOCcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke32000ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TC2wljWxn8AEV1KgIBn53mUR41Setzhw52aXlh1u2wgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd8568ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QllxtR0gF-S06r9iIlViPS_7QBn3P8DhlZlJde1B1Ygƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;wbQKrQx6StyPsIqV2J5TR9HXzrTe0fPqVklV4GwZi-Mƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka4ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;StTXWIEIHVr-P67d7epX3Vh4K5C2KuAIrfAMfd0K2soƒdpairƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;D-v1pYAzs_rfsRn0QvNcDOUdP34c_YHn86tGNrc4F5Eƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyñ{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"Hedge all non-title \"will\" in body and metadata; resubmit.","summary":"The manuscript is substantively sound â€” sources are correctly cited, factual claims are traceable, the geometry analysis is faithfully rendered, and attribution is visible throughout. Two bounded, mechanical repairs are required before publication: (1) the writer model used \"will\" in body text and metadata in violation of the explicit writing law, and (2) the description and summary metadata carry certainty language for interpretive claims, which the deterministic modality gate flagged.","requiredRepairs":["Replace every instance of \"will\" in the body text with a hedged or present-tense alternative, as required by the writing law (\"NEVER use the word 'will' outside a quoted source title\"). Specific instances: lede â€” \"Spain will face Argentina\" â†’ \"Spain face Argentina\" or \"Spain faces Argentina\"; last section â€” \"Argentina will study their opponents' tactics\" â†’ \"Argentina is expected to study\" or remove the predictive framing; \"geometry will play a prominent role\" â†’ \"geometry may play a prominent role\" (the source's own \"may even prove\" modality should be preserved here in any case).","Revise the `description` and `summary` metadata fields to remove certainty language from interpretive claims. The deterministic gate (attention-preserve-modality, severity: warning) explicitly flags these surfaces. Replace \"Spain will face Argentina\" in the description and summary with present-tense or hedged language, and ensure no interpretive claim in those fields is presented with greater certainty than the source warrants. The factual kernel (match at MetLife, both sides unbeaten, tournament credentials) may remain; the forward-looking framing must be hedged."],"report":"## Source and Evidence Audit\n\nThe primary licensed source is the Conversation article by Conor Boland (published 2026-07-17, URL: https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792). All major analytical claims â€” the triangle logic, Porro's 58th-minute goal, MbappÃ©'s \"lack of communication\" quote (via Reuters), the Tex Winter/Chicago Bulls comparison (1987â€“1998), the PLOS ONE passing-network research, the Nature player-tracking data â€” are traceable to the primary source and reproduced faithfully. The manuscript does not invent facts, numbers, people, or causal mechanisms. The faithfulness-gate number (10.1371% in the DOI) appears correctly in the PLOS ONE link. Attribution is visible in every factual paragraph; citations appear as grammatical prose links rather than dropped references.\n\nContext 1 (tactically-complete article) is used narrowly and correctly: the \"What Argentina must answer\" section draws on it for the compact-defending/counter-attack description and the both-sides-unbeaten fact in the lede. Neither use stretches Context 1 beyond what its text warrants.\n\nThe downstream links to Reuters, AP News, Nature, PLOS ONE, and the Basketball Hall of Fame are all links that appear in the primary source itself and are used appropriately. No context items are fused, and no attribution is transferred across sources.\n\nConor Boland's disclosure (no conflicting affiliations) appears in the source text; the manuscript does not need to reproduce it but correctly treats Boland as an author, not a subject of the story.\n\n## Deterministic Gate Findings\n\nThe gate reports `publishable: true` and `draft: true`, with two warnings. Both warnings identify real problems:\n\n**`attention-preserve-modality`**: The description and summary metadata use \"will face\" and present certainty about a match outcome that is, at article-writing time, a future event. The source uses \"will\" because it is pre-event copy, but the writing law overrides source modality on this point: \"a vision is never a prophecy: write 'might' or 'could'. NEVER use the word 'will' outside a quoted source title.\" The description and summary are not quoted titles; they must be hedged.\n\n**`demand-lens-missing-h2`**: The search-demand lens (\"geometry football cup final\") is substantively expressed throughout the body â€” the article is entirely about football geometry at a World Cup final. The gate flags that no level-two heading naturally signals it. This is a minor structural note; the body content satisfies the demand lens without forcing the exact phrase, which the writing law explicitly permits. This warning does not independently require a repair.\n\n## Body Text \"Will\" Violations\n\nBeyond the metadata, the body itself uses \"will\" outside quoted source titles in three places:\n\n1. Lede: \"Spain will face Argentina in the FIFA World Cup final this Sunday at MetLife Stadium in New Jersey.\" The source says \"Spain will play Argentina,\" but the writing law does not allow \"will\" in body copy outside a quoted title. The lede should read \"Spain face Argentina\" (present tense) or similar.\n\n2. Last section: \"the analysis that mapped Spain's semi-final geometry concludes that Argentina will study their opponents' tactics closely before the final.\" This is a paraphrase of source predictive language. Should be \"Argentina are expected to study\" or restructured to remove the auxiliary.\n\n3. Last section: \"that geometry will play a prominent role in the championship match.\" The source says \"geometry will still play a prominent role â€“ and may even prove a deciding factor.\" The body correctly hedges \"may prove\" on the second clause but not the first. Should be \"geometry may play a prominent role.\"\n\nThese are mechanical violations of an explicit writing rule. They do not reflect a sourcing or assignment problem; the underlying reporting is solid. The writer model had the rule and applied it inconsistently.\n\n## Scope of Repair\n\nBoth required repairs are bounded to output text and metadata. No fresh reporting is needed. No structural replay of the assignment is needed. The thesis, analysis, sourcing, citation hygiene, section structure, headline, and all substantive factual claims are publication-ready once the modality corrections are applied.\n\n## Pipeline Diagnosis\n\nFailure layer: writer-model. The assignment and sources support the article as written; the sourcing layer did not withhold evidence; the instruction layer stated the \"no will\" rule clearly. The writer model violated that rule in three body locations and in both metadata summary fields. This is a contained output error, not a structural deficiency in the reporting packet or assignment."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_6wBPUZchTwl02uVWOm-ilz7e9VEnI6JK7LUHHdn6AYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$84c4697a-932e-4ada-88f6-164cf71d53d3ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;x5827MhP9IZ7FXJoRYUlKTM3HStp-XHTixJKxYQOAwwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhtool_useƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;D3k5QQWV6X4GfHhBNkTzLOa6e6DncFzXXynFY4_2NDkƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;tzqkdxrVhsZB3xUdpMMBU-w134llUwnECCHE6aQo_ZQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarklwriter-modelƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;FuPSgCBh4yh5j2nysd-hP-bGf5rfI2FSGOVhG_7t4u0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx:Hedge all non-title "will" in body and metadata; resubmit.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Nksf_W410nQsYpJAD6sMQy2s2kM5QUM1IrW21b6pglAƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyV## Source and Evidence Audit

The primary licensed source is the Conversation article by Conor Boland (published 2026-07-17, URL: https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792). All major analytical claims â€” the triangle logic, Porro's 58th-minute goal, MbappÃ©'s "lack of communication" quote (via Reuters), the Tex Winter/Chicago Bulls comparison (1987â€“1998), the PLOS ONE passing-network research, the Nature player-tracking data â€” are traceable to the primary source and reproduced faithfully. The manuscript does not invent facts, numbers, people, or causal mechanisms. The faithfulness-gate number (10.1371% in the DOI) appears correctly in the PLOS ONE link. Attribution is visible in every factual paragraph; citations appear as grammatical prose links rather than dropped references.

Context 1 (tactically-complete article) is used narrowly and correctly: the "What Argentina must answer" section draws on it for the compact-defending/counter-attack description and the both-sides-unbeaten fact in the lede. Neither use stretches Context 1 beyond what its text warrants.

The downstream links to Reuters, AP News, Nature, PLOS ONE, and the Basketball Hall of Fame are all links that appear in the primary source itself and are used appropriately. No context items are fused, and no attribution is transferred across sources.

Conor Boland's disclosure (no conflicting affiliations) appears in the source text; the manuscript does not need to reproduce it but correctly treats Boland as an author, not a subject of the story.

## Deterministic Gate Findings

The gate reports `publishable: true` and `draft: true`, with two warnings. Both warnings identify real problems:

**`attention-preserve-modality`**: The description and summary metadata use "will face" and present certainty about a match outcome that is, at article-writing time, a future event. The source uses "will" because it is pre-event copy, but the writing law overrides source modality on this point: "a vision is never a prophecy: write 'might' or 'could'. NEVER use the word 'will' outside a quoted source title." The description and summary are not quoted titles; they must be hedged.

**`demand-lens-missing-h2`**: The search-demand lens ("geometry football cup final") is substantively expressed throughout the body â€” the article is entirely about football geometry at a World Cup final. The gate flags that no level-two heading naturally signals it. This is a minor structural note; the body content satisfies the demand lens without forcing the exact phrase, which the writing law explicitly permits. This warning does not independently require a repair.

## Body Text "Will" Violations

Beyond the metadata, the body itself uses "will" outside quoted source titles in three places:

1. Lede: "Spain will face Argentina in the FIFA World Cup final this Sunday at MetLife Stadium in New Jersey." The source says "Spain will play Argentina," but the writing law does not allow "will" in body copy outside a quoted title. The lede should read "Spain face Argentina" (present tense) or similar.

2. Last section: "the analysis that mapped Spain's semi-final geometry concludes that Argentina will study their opponents' tactics closely before the final." This is a paraphrase of source predictive language. Should be "Argentina are expected to study" or restructured to remove the auxiliary.

3. Last section: "that geometry will play a prominent role in the championship match." The source says "geometry will still play a prominent role â€“ and may even prove a deciding factor." The body correctly hedges "may prove" on the second clause but not the first. Should be "geometry may play a prominent role."

These are mechanical violations of an explicit writing rule. They do not reflect a sourcing or assignment problem; the underlying reporting is solid. The writer model had the rule and applied it inconsistently.

## Scope of Repair

Both required repairs are bounded to output text and metadata. No fresh reporting is needed. No structural replay of the assignment is needed. The thesis, analysis, sourcing, citation hygiene, section structure, headline, and all substantive factual claims are publication-ready once the modality corrections are applied.

## Pipeline Diagnosis

